D = "ivx_interpersonal"
S = "Sullivan, The Psychiatric Interview (1954)"
ITEMS = [
dict(d=D, c="ivx sullivan vocal not verbal", m="eye", l=3, hard=True, s=S+", chapter I",
q="Sullivan defined the psychiatric interview as primarily “vocal” rather than “verbal” communication. What did he mean?",
a="The sound of speech (tone, rate, hesitation) carries much of the meaning",
ae="Sullivan argued that intonation, rate of speech, difficulty in enunciation and shifts in tone are dependable clues to what a person means. A patient may state facts correctly while sounding different as the talk approaches a sensitive area, and that change of sound is the cue to follow.",
w=[("Written questionnaires should be avoided in favor of speech",
"Sullivan was not making a point about questionnaires. His contrast was between the words themselves and the vocal qualities around them."),
("Only what the patient says aloud should be recorded as data",
"He meant the opposite: the non-word qualities of speech are essential data, not just the spoken propositions."),
("Interviewers should talk more than patients to guide them",
"Vocal does not refer to who talks more. It refers to the paralinguistic layer of communication.")],
x="His example: a journeyman electrician describing union rules sounds different when he reaches dues he once pocketed. Sullivan shows mild interest exactly at the point of tonal difference, and the confession follows. Modern paralinguistics research (prosody, pauses, speech rate) has since confirmed how much information the voice carries.",
h="Sullivan: “Listen to the music, not just the lyrics.” The voice changes key near the secret."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx sullivan ninety degrees", m="eye", l=3, hard=True, s=S+", chapter I",
q="For more than seven years, Sullivan sat at an angle of ninety degrees to the people he interviewed and looked ahead rather than at them. Why?",
a="Shy, scrutiny-sensitive patients, especially with schizophrenia, are embarrassed by being stared at",
ae="Sullivan observed that patients with schizophrenia were shy, low in self-esteem and sensitive to being “looked in the eye.” By not staring, he reduced their anxiety. He relied on peripheral vision for movement and developed his listening to catch what most people think they can only see.",
w=[("He believed eye contact contaminated free association",
"This resembles the logic of the analytic couch, but Sullivan’s stated reason concerned the shyness of patients with schizophrenia."),
("He had poor eyesight and could not read faces well",
"Nothing in his account suggests a visual impairment. It was a deliberate technique."),
("He thought the eyes reveal lies and wanted patients to feel safe lying",
"He called the belief that eyes are windows of the soul misguided, and his aim was to lower anxiety, not to permit lying.")],
x="This anticipates modern advice for paranoid and psychotic patients: sit at an angle, avoid prolonged direct gaze, and do not crowd the person. Sullivan added that most clues to what people really mean reach us through the ears.",
h="Ninety degrees: “Side-by-side seating, ears wide open.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx sullivan sounds amazing", m="say", l=3, hard=True, s=S+", chapter I",
q="A patient says of a friend, “He’s my dearest friend; he hasn’t a hostile impulse toward me!” Following Sullivan, which response best opens the area?",
a="“Is that so? It sounds amazing.”",
ae="Sullivan noticed that overly conventional or idealized statements usually hide something. Saying it “sounds amazing” puts the patient slightly on the spot, so he elaborates, and the interviewer can then ask whether the friend ever did anything unfortunate in its effect. In Sullivan’s case, the friend had run off with the patient’s wife.",
w=[("“That’s wonderful. It is rare to have such a friend.”",
"This agrees with the idealization and closes the door to what lies behind it."),
("“I think you may be idealizing him to avoid anger.”",
"This is a premature interpretation. Sullivan let the patient discover the discrepancy from his own data."),
("“Let’s move on to your work history now.”",
"Moving on misses the signal that the statement is too conventional to be taken at face value.")],
x="Sullivan’s principle: the more conventional a person’s statements, the less sure you can be of what they mean. Mild surprise or gentle irony (“And doubtless happily?” about a marriage) invites explanation without accusation.",
h="Too-perfect statements: “Sounds amazing!” Make the idealization explain itself."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx sullivan setting of trouble", m="ask", l=3, hard=True, s=S+", chapter I",
q="A patient insists his “nervous condition” is hereditary and has nothing to do with other people. Which approach did Sullivan recommend?",
a="Ask in what settings it occurs, and let him notice who is present",
ae="Sullivan advised against telling such patients that their problem is interpersonal. Instead he asked when and in what setting the trouble occurs. Patients then produce instances involving particular people, and only then does the interviewer note the people with whom it never happens. The patient reaches the interpersonal insight from his own data.",
w=[("Explain early that most neuroses are interpersonal in origin",
"Sullivan thought even experienced psychiatrists should not expose the patient to that stress. He preferred letting the data speak."),
("Accept the hereditary explanation to protect the alliance",
"Accepting it uncritically abandons the inquiry into the interpersonal context."),
("Order genetic testing to settle the question",
"This misses the clinical point, which is about discovering the interpersonal pattern.")],
x="This is a model of guided discovery decades before CBT named it. It also mirrors functional analysis: the context reveals the pattern. Sullivan: the psychiatric interview is designed to uncover difficulties in living that the patient does not clearly understand.",
h="Not “It’s interpersonal”: ask “Where and with whom does it happen?”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx sullivan quid pro quo", m="eye", l=3, hard=True, s=S+", chapter I",
q="Sullivan criticized “one-sided interrogation.” What, in his view, keeps a patient communicative through a disturbing interview?",
a="Learning early that he will get something useful about how he lives",
ae="Sullivan called this the quid pro quo: the patient tolerates the discomfort of being straightforward about failures because he senses he is learning something useful. Interviews that ignore the informant’s anxiety and give no clue to the meaning of the information produce cautious, guarded patients.",
w=[("Being reassured repeatedly that nothing he says will be judged",
"Reassurance alone was not Sullivan’s answer. Patients need to experience benefit, not only safety."),
("Being allowed to speak without interruption for the whole hour",
"Sullivan actually discouraged irrelevant or repetitive talk. Free rein was not the motivator."),
("Being told the diagnosis in the first ten minutes",
"Early labeling was not his point. Benefit comes from understanding, not a label.")],
x="This anticipates modern ideas of therapeutic assessment (Finn) and the collaborative interview. If the patient leaves with more clarity about himself, Sullivan considered the essential goal achieved, even in a single session.",
h="Quid pro quo: “They give data, they must get understanding.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx sullivan parataxic distortion", m="eye", l=2, s=S+", chapter I",
q="What did Sullivan call the process in which the interviewee reacts to the interviewer as if he were a quite different, partly fictitious person?",
a="Parataxic distortion",
ae="Parataxic distortion is Sullivan’s term for responding to a fictitious construction of the other person rather than to the actual person. It occurs in everyday relationships and especially in the interview. Sullivan added that it may be an obscure attempt to communicate a grave problem, which the therapist needs to grasp.",
w=[("Selective inattention",
"Selective inattention is not noticing anxiety-laden experience. It is not about misperceiving the other person."),
("Security operation",
"Security operations are maneuvers to reduce anxiety. They can accompany parataxic distortion but are a different concept."),
("Projective identification",
"Projective identification comes from Klein and later authors, and includes pressure on the recipient. Sullivan’s term is broader and descriptive.")],
x="Parataxic distortion overlaps with transference but is framed interpersonally, without Freud’s drive theory. Sullivan also described developmental modes of experience: prototaxic, parataxic and syntaxic. Syntaxic experience is consensually validated, shared meaning.",
h="Parataxic = “a para-person sits in your chair.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx sullivan warning before question", m="say", l=2, s=S+", chapter II",
q="According to Sullivan, how does an expert interviewer show respect before asking about a topic where the patient feels insecure?",
a="By giving a brief preliminary signal so the patient can brace himself",
ae="Sullivan described making a small preliminary movement that shows awareness that a question will be unpleasant, while making clear the information is necessary. This little warning respects the patient’s sense of security, which he saw as the first element of expertness.",
w=[("By asking the question suddenly so that defenses cannot form",
"Abrupt questions have uses, but Sullivan described the respectful norm as warning the patient first."),
("By avoiding the topic until the patient raises it himself",
"Sullivan insisted the expert is entitled to relevant data and goes after it."),
("By telling the patient that the question is not important",
"Downplaying a necessary question is misleading and does not respect the patient.")],
x="When the patient is anxious about something entirely ordinary, Sullivan sometimes replied in effect, “Dear me! I never heard anybody talk honestly who didn’t mention that.” Modern accented transitions and normalizing statements descend from these moves.",
h="Sullivan’s courtesy: “Signal the bump before the road gets rough.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx sullivan secrecy paranoid", m="say", l=3, hard=True, s=S+", chapter II",
q="A paranoid patient refuses to say anything about his troublesome neighbor but wants your help with him. Which response follows Sullivan?",
a="“Am I to understand your difficulty with him without any information at all?”",
ae="Sullivan put the dilemma plainly. The patient may feel the psychiatrist should understand anyway, but stated that way it sounds odd. If secrecy persists, the interviewer simply notes that he cannot be useful without data, which itself reveals remarkable secrets, sometimes secret even from the patient.",
w=[("“I understand completely; we don’t need to discuss him.”",
"This abandons the expert’s legitimate claim to relevant data."),
("“Your secrecy is a symptom of paranoia.”",
"Labeling the secrecy is likely to increase suspicion and end engagement."),
("“If you won’t tell me, I can’t see you anymore.”",
"An ultimatum is coercive. Sullivan stated the limits of usefulness without threatening.")],
x="Sullivan held that the expert need not argue about his right to data. He presents it as a working assumption of the inquiry and, if challenged, explains it once, plainly. The patient’s difficulty in accepting it is itself diagnostic.",
h="Secrecy: “Can I help with no information at all?” Let the absurdity speak."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx sullivan curiosity forgone", m="eye", l=2, s=S+", chapter II",
q="Sullivan said the expert interviewer “foregoes the satisfaction of any curiosity” without technical reason. Why does this help the patient?",
a="It shows the interviewer is seeking what ails him, not amusement",
ae="Sullivan described actively cutting off accounts once the important information is heard, even when the rest would be “thrilling.” Patients appreciate being spared harrowing detail and realize that the doctor was trying to understand them rather than entertain himself.",
w=[("It shortens interviews so more patients can be seen",
"Efficiency is a side effect. Sullivan’s reason was the patient’s experience of the interviewer’s purpose."),
("It prevents the interviewer from being called as a witness",
"Legal concerns were not his reasoning."),
("It forces the patient to volunteer details spontaneously",
"He was not trying to provoke spontaneous disclosure. He was limiting intrusion.")],
x="Sullivan also warned that intense curiosity about sexual life or drinking is an unfortunate ingredient in any interview. Clinicians can check themselves: is this question for the patient’s benefit or my interest?",
h="Sullivan: “Ask what you need, not what you want to know.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx sullivan banalities", m="say", l=2, s=S+", chapter II",
q="Sullivan had harsh words for psychiatrists who use terms like “mother-fixation” with patients. What was his objection?",
a="Such banalities condense rich, specific detail into meaningless labels",
ae="Sullivan argued that cultural bromides and psychiatric banalities cloud issues. In his experience, each “mother-fixation” he encountered came with a wealth of specific detail that could not be meaningfully condensed under a label.",
w=[("The term was not scientifically accepted at the time",
"His objection was clinical and communicative, not about scientific acceptance."),
("Patients might be offended by any mention of their mother",
"He discussed mothers in detail; the issue was the empty label."),
("It reveals the psychiatrist’s psychoanalytic training",
"Sullivan was trained in the psychoanalytic school himself. The issue was meaninglessness.")],
x="He also warned against giving tacit consent to delusions or grievous errors by staying silent about them. Together these rules ask the interviewer to say less that is empty and more that is precise.",
h="Banality ban: “Labels are fog; details are light.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx sullivan expected role", m="ask", l=3, hard=True, s=S+", chapter II",
q="Sullivan argued that a patient’s culturally shaped expectations of the expert must be considered in the first meeting. What happens if they are ignored?",
a="The patient will likely not return, even if offered something “better”",
ae="Sullivan wrote that what a client is taught to expect is what he should get, or any departure should be carefully arranged. A patient who comes for contentment will not follow you to learning a trade unless you first take his expectation into account.",
w=[("The patient will become more motivated by the surprise",
"Surprise is rarely motivating in a first meeting. Sullivan predicted disengagement."),
("Nothing changes, because expectations are unconscious anyway",
"He saw expectations as decisive in the earliest stage of the relationship."),
("The referring doctor’s endorsement will compensate",
"He said that referral may get the patient onto the subway but does nothing to establish the expert-client relationship.")],
x="Sullivan credited Adolf Meyer for the phrase “here and now”: expertness must be demonstrated in the interview itself. Respect and sensitivity to the patient’s sense of worth are the first marks of expertness.",
h="Expectations first: “Meet them where they came to go, then lead.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx sullivan bellevue advice", m="say", l=3, hard=True, s=S+", chapter I",
q="A woman with persecutory beliefs about a colleague has been controlling growing rage for weeks. Sullivan ended the brief interview with a specific instruction. Which?",
a="If she feels impelled to act physically, first seek voluntary admission",
ae="Sullivan told her that, both as a psychiatrist and as a member of society, if she felt impelled to do something physical to her persecutor, she should first go to Bellevue and apply for voluntary admission for a few days. She protested, but he judged she benefited from that clear ending.",
w=[("Confront the colleague directly to test the belief",
"Encouraging confrontation with a persecutor risks violence."),
("Stop seeing her pastor, who was reinforcing the delusion",
"Sullivan explicitly valued the help her pastor gave and did not try to replace it."),
("Begin intensive psychoanalysis immediately",
"In a brief consultation, he gave a concrete safety prescription instead.")],
x="This is a model of a brief risk intervention: respect for existing supports, no argument with the delusion, and a clear, concrete safety plan tied to the specific risk (rage at an identified person).",
h="Sullivan’s exit line: “Before you act, admit yourself.”"),
]
